course: dictionary
lesson: 14316
title: When a rule says you must
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Obligatorily is a formal word, so it may look hard, but the word parts help. Obligatory means required, and the -ly ending shows it is an adverb, a word that tells how something is done. So obligatorily means "in a way that a rule or law requires".
explain: «The rules say we must wash our hands, so we obligatorily wash them before lunch.» Read the words around it: the rule says we must. Obligatorily tells you the washing is not a choice. A rule makes it happen.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. Try "we have to wash them before lunch" or "it is compulsory to wash them". Both still make sense, so you have found the right meaning.
fact: Obligatorily means in a way that a rule or law requires.
fact: Obligatorily is an adverb, so it tells how something is done.
fact: It is a formal word, found in rules, law and exam language.
fact: «Compulsory» and «required» mean much the same thing, and they are easier to use in everyday speech.
fact: Something done obligatorily is not a choice: if you are free to choose, it is not obligatory.
exercise: «The rules say we must wash our hands, so we obligatorily wash them before lunch.» What does obligatorily mean? | Only sometimes, if we are hungry | Because we feel like it | Quickly and quietly | *Because a rule says we must
exercise: Ruby says obligatorily is just a fancy way of saying "sometimes". Is she right? | No, it means happy | Yes, it means very quickly | *No, it means required by a rule | Yes, it means now and then
exercise: «Seat belts are obligatorily worn in the car.» What must happen? | Wearing seat belts is optional | *Wearing seat belts is required | Seat belts are sold in shops | Seat belts are very old
test: «Pupils are obligatorily asked to wear a coat when it snows.» What does obligatorily mean? | Because they are cold only | *Because a rule says they must | Because it is a sunny day | Because the teacher is nice
test: Which phrase could replace obligatorily without changing the meaning? | *As a rule requires it | Quite by chance | Only on Fridays | If they fancy it
exam: «Entry to the lab is obligatorily restricted to staff.» What does obligatorily tell us about the restriction? | *It is required by the rules | It is free for everyone | It happens only once | It is a choice
